Streams API
-------------

Streams provide us a way to process collection of data.

Instead of manually writing loops, streams provide operations like
filter(), sorted(), map(), min(), max(), reduce() etc to process the collection of objects easily
in a more readable, easier to understand and concise code.

filter() is an intermediate operation; it sets up the filtering step. forEach() is a terminal operation;
it triggers processing and prints the results. Streams are generally processed when a terminal operation
is invoked.

map() vs filter()
---------------------

filter() - removes elements that don't satisfy the condition.

            .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)

map() - transforms elements - Transforms each element into another value; it doesn't filter elements out.

        .map(name -> name.toUpperCase())

Simple way :

filter() = Which elements should I keep?
map() = What should I change each element into?

reduce() - reduces the list of values into a single value, usually used with operations like sum, avg, etc

           // To find sum of values from a list
           Integer res = nums.stream()
                            .reduce(0, (a,b) -> (a+b));


Operations & their Purpose
----------------------------

forEach() - Perform an action on each element
filter() - Select matching elements
map() - Transform each element
reduce() - Combine elements into one result
count() - Count the elements
min() - Find the smallest number - stream().min
max() - Find the largest number  - stream().max()
sorted() - Sorts in natural order (ascending for integers).
sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder()) -	Sorts in reverse order (descending for integers).
distinct() - operation removes duplicate elements from a stream.
findFirst() — gets the first matching character.
orElse(null) — returns null if no character is found.